{
  "abstract": "A directed edge from an odd cofactor to its triple represents the implication that convergence of the cofactor to 1 implies convergence of its triple. For routes at height $Cn$ with cofactor ceiling $Kn$, write $\\lambda=\\min(C,3K)\\ge64$. Every sufficient set has lower natural density $\\Omega(1/(C\\lambda))$. One set of odd cofactors prime to three supplies both directions between 1 and every target with upper density $O(1/(C\\lambda))$. The construction permits finite deletions and has an exponential local cost tail at ordinary component minima. A nine-digit ternary modulus gives finite estimates for growing caps. For $0\\le\\beta\\le\\alpha$ and $\\alpha+(6/25)\\beta\\le 1-h_2(\\log_3 2)$, the two power budgets have sharp catalogue count of order $Y^{(1-\\alpha)/(1+\\beta)}$ through cofactor $Y$. Distinct exiting components also give lower counts for the height of ordinary joins of a cofactor and its triple. In every sufficiently large octave we construct a unit cofactor $q$ whose two ordinary paths meet in $O(\\log q)$ steps and whose least joining height is of order $q^{\\log_2 3}$. The meeting proves the tripling implication on explicit progressions. The implications at the remaining selected cofactors are open.",
